00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,336
听：Testing a New Packaging Idea.

2
00:00:03,336 --> 00:00:06,672
跟读：Testing a New Packaging Idea.

3
00:00:06,672 --> 00:00:30,840
听：In a college workshop, Amir developed a container intended to replace some disposable packaging. His first prototype looked promising, but appearance was not enough. He needed to find out whether the material could protect its contents during an ordinary delivery and whether customers could reuse it easily.

4
00:00:30,840 --> 00:00:55,008
跟读：In a college workshop, Amir developed a container intended to replace some disposable packaging. His first prototype looked promising, but appearance was not enough. He needed to find out whether the material could protect its contents during an ordinary delivery and whether customers could reuse it easily.

5
00:00:55,008 --> 00:01:18,096
听：Amir designed an experiment with help from a tutor. The material was the main variable, while the size of each container remained constant. He recorded a hypothesis before beginning the trial. This helped him avoid changing the question simply because he preferred one result.

6
00:01:18,096 --> 00:01:41,184
跟读：Amir designed an experiment with help from a tutor. The material was the main variable, while the size of each container remained constant. He recorded a hypothesis before beginning the trial. This helped him avoid changing the question simply because he preferred one result.

7
00:01:41,184 --> 00:02:09,792
听：After several tests, one design performed better than the others. However, the sample was small, and durability under different conditions remained uncertain. Had he tested only the strongest container, his conclusion would have been misleading. He repeated the procedure and kept a record of unsuccessful attempts as well as successful ones.

8
00:02:09,792 --> 00:02:38,400
跟读：After several tests, one design performed better than the others. However, the sample was small, and durability under different conditions remained uncertain. Had he tested only the strongest container, his conclusion would have been misleading. He repeated the procedure and kept a record of unsuccessful attempts as well as successful ones.

9
00:02:38,400 --> 00:03:10,992
听：The team then considered the environmental impact across the product's lifecycle. A reusable container still required resources to manufacture and transport. Amir decided that feasibility involved cost, convenience and likely reuse, not merely a successful demonstration. He presented the limitations openly and suggested further research before making a broad commercial claim.

